---
description: Plans must isolate work on a dedicated git branch, commit finished work there, and verify clean mergeability without merging
alwaysApply: true
---
Plan Git Branch Rules:

When writing or executing a plan, ALWAYS include and follow these git workflow requirements:

1. Branch isolation
   - Create a dedicated feature/fix branch before any plan work begins
   - Do ALL plan implementation, commits, and verification on that branch
   - NEVER commit plan work directly to `main`/`master` (or the repo's default base branch)

2. Commit finished work (required before a plan is done)
   - After implementation, commit all plan work to the created branch
   - Do not leave plan changes unstaged or uncommitted
   - A plan is not finished until its work is committed on the work branch
   - Plan-finish commits do not wait for a separate "please commit" request
   - Follow normal commit safety (no secrets, no hook-skip, no force-push)

3. Verification stage (required in every plan)
   - Confirm the branch is up to date with the base branch (fetch + rebase or merge-base check as appropriate)
   - Verify the branch can merge cleanly into the base branch (e.g. dry-run / conflict check)
   - Run relevant tests/checks needed to show the branch is merge-ready
   - Do NOT perform the actual merge into the base branch
   - Leave merge/PR merge for the user (or a later explicit request)

4. Plan text
   - Explicitly state the branch name (or how it will be named)
   - Explicitly state that finished work will be committed on that branch
   - Explicitly list mergeability verification steps
   - Explicitly state that verification must not merge
